---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Sources from '../components/Sources.astro';
import { ORG } from '../config';
import { breadcrumbs, organization } from '../data/schema';
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'About', href: '/about' }];
---
<Base title="About Sugar Depth and Martian Dynamics" description="Sugar Depth is made by Martian Dynamics LLC in Leander, Texas. Why it exists, how its rules are sourced, and what it does not claim." path="/about" jsonLd={[organization, breadcrumbs(crumbs)]}>
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <span class="kicker">About</span>
    <h1>Label transparency, with a sense of humor.</h1>
    <p class="lead">Sugar Depth is a food-label scanner made by {ORG.name}, a small company in {ORG.locality}, Texas. It reads the ingredient list on a package and calls out added sugars and sweeteners by their real names.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap page-body prose">
    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>Ingredient lists are honest, but they're written in a language few people read fluently. Sugar appears under dozens of names, and sweeteners that aren't sugar at all can sit behind a "0 g sugar" panel. Sugar Depth reads the list for you and says, in one word, whether anything sweet was added.</p>
    <p>The tone is satirical on purpose. Sugar's disguises are a little absurd ("evaporated cane juice"), and pointing that out is more memorable than another chart. The facts underneath are not a joke: every label rule described on this site links to FDA guidance or federal regulations.</p>
    <p class="needs">[needs your input] A sentence or two in your own words about why you started Sugar Depth.</p>

    <h2>What Sugar Depth does not claim</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It is not medical or nutrition advice, and it doesn't diagnose or treat anything.</li>
      <li>Its verdicts are its own preferences about sweeteners. For example, FDA permits every sweetener named on this site; Sugar Depth simply chooses to flag them.</li>
      <li>It doesn't check allergens.</li>
      <li>It doesn't rate products overall, count calories or keep a product database.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the rules are sourced</h2>
    <p>The rules are published in full on <a href="/how-it-works">how it works</a>. How US labels treat each ingredient comes from these sources:</p>
    <Sources heading="Primary sources" keys={['addedSugarsRule', 'addedSugarsQA', 'allulose', 'rareSugars', 'maltodextrin', 'fiber', 'nutritionLabel', 'sweeteners', 'ecj']} />

    <h2>Company</h2>
    <p>{ORG.name}<br />{ORG.locality}, Texas, United States<br /><a href={`mailto:${ORG.email}`}>{ORG.email}</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>
